window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-physics-scalars-vectors-types-addition-resolution-dot-cross-products"] = `
<div class="revision-card" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; line-height:1.6; max-width:900px; margin:auto; padding:20px; background:#f9f9f9; border:1px solid #ddd;">
    <h1 style="text-align:center; color:#2c3e50;">Scalars &amp; Vectors – Types, Addition, Resolution, Dot &amp; Cross Products</h1>
    
    <h2 style="color:#34495e;">1. Quick Overview</h2>
    <p>In mechanics, every physical quantity is either a <strong>scalar</strong> (magnitude only) or a <strong>vector</strong> (magnitude + direction). Mastery of vector algebra is essential for NDA, CDS, AFCAT and all engineering‑physics papers.</p>
    
    <h2 style="color:#34495e;">2. Scalars vs. Vectors – Definition &amp; Examples</h2>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background:#e2e8f0;">
                <th style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Aspect</th>
                <th style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Scalar</th>
                <th style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Vector</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;"><strong>Definition</strong></td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Quantity described by a single number (and unit) only.</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Quantity described by a magnitude and a specific direction.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;"><strong>Notation</strong></td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Plain italic (e.g., <em>m</em>, <em>T</em>)</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Boldface or arrow (e.g., <strong>v</strong> or <span style="text-decoration:overline;">v</span>)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;"><strong>Examples</strong></td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">
                    <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px;">
                        <li>Mass (kg)</li>
                        <li>Temperature (°C)</li>
                        <li>Time (s)</li>
                        <li>Energy (J)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">
                    <ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px;">
                        <li>Displacement <strong>r</strong> (m)</li>
                        <li>Velocity <strong>v</strong> (m s<sup>‑1</sup>)</li>
                        <li>Acceleration <strong>a</strong> (m s<sup>‑2</sup>)</li>
                        <li>Force <strong>F</strong> (N)</li>
                        <li>Momentum <strong>p</strong> (kg m s<sup>‑1</sup>)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;"><strong>Operations</strong></td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Add, subtract, multiply, divide (ordinary arithmetic).</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Add, subtract (vector algebra), dot product, cross product, scalar multiplication.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    
    <h2 style="color:#34495e;">3. Types of Vectors – Classification</h2>
    <p>Vectors are further classified based on their origin, constancy, and physical meaning.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Position Vector (<strong>r</strong>)</strong> – Extends from the origin O to a point P. <br>Magnitude = distance OP.</li>
        <li><strong>Displacement Vector (<strong>Δr</strong>)</strong> – Difference between final and initial position vectors: <strong>Δr = r₂ − r₁</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Velocity Vector (<strong>v</strong>)</strong> – Time‑rate of change of displacement: <strong>v = d r / d t</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Acceleration Vector (<strong>a</strong>)</strong> – Time‑rate of change of velocity: <strong>a = d v / d t</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Force Vector (<strong>F</strong>)</strong> – Interaction causing acceleration (Newton’s 2nd law: <strong>F = m a</strong>).</li>
        <li><strong>Momentum Vector (<strong>p</strong>)</strong> – Product of mass and velocity: <strong>p = m v</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Angular Quantities</strong> – <strong>Angular velocity ω</strong>, <strong>Angular acceleration α</strong>, <strong>Torque τ = r × F</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Unit Vectors</strong> – Denoted by <strong>î, ĵ, k̂</strong> along x, y, z axes respectively.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">3.1 Historical Nuggets</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><span style="color: var(--warning);">1687</span> – Isaac Newton publishes <em>Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica</em>, laying the foundation of vector‑based dynamics.</li>
        <li><span style="color: var(--warning);">1805</span> – William Rowan Hamilton introduces quaternions, the first systematic treatment of three‑dimensional vectors.</li>
        <li><span style="color: var(--warning);">1845</span> – Josiah Willard Gibbs formalises modern vector analysis (dot &amp; cross products) used today.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2 style="color:#34495e;">4. Vector Addition &amp; Subtraction</h2>
    <p>Two fundamental graphical methods are used in exams: <strong>Tip‑to‑Tail (Polygon) Method</strong> and <strong>Parallelogram Method</strong>. Both give the same resultant.</p>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">4.1 Tip‑to‑Tail (Polygon) Method</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Place the tail of the second vector at the tip of the first.</li>
        <li>Continue for all vectors in the given order.</li>
        <li>The resultant is the vector drawn from the tail of the first vector to the tip of the last.</li>
    </ol>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">4.2 Parallelogram Method</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Place both vectors so that their tails coincide at a common point.</li>
        <li>Complete the parallelogram formed by the two vectors.</li>
        <li>The diagonal through the common tail is the resultant.</li>
    </ol>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">4.3 Component (Algebraic) Method – The Exam Favourite</h3>
    <p>For rapid calculations, resolve each vector into orthogonal components (usually x‑ and y‑axes) and add component‑wise.</p>
    <pre style="background:#eef; padding:10px; border-left:4px solid #3498db;">
Resultant R = ΣRₓ î + ΣR_y ĵ
where Rₓ = Σ (R_i cosθ_i) ,   R_y = Σ (R_i sinθ_i)
    </pre>
    <p><strong>Key tip:</strong> Always keep a consistent sign convention (right/up = +, left/down = –).</p>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">4.4 Vector Subtraction</h3>
    <p>Subtracting <strong>B</strong> from <strong>A</strong> is equivalent to adding <strong>−B</strong>. Graphically, reverse the direction of <strong>B</strong> and then use the addition rules.</p>
    
    <h2 style="color:#34495e;">5. Resolution of Vectors into Components</h2>
    <p>Resolution is the reverse of addition – breaking a vector into two (or three) mutually perpendicular components.</p>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">5.1 Two‑Dimensional Resolution</h3>
    <p>Given a vector **V** of magnitude <span style="color: var(--warning);">V</span> making an angle <span style="color: var(--warning);">θ</span> with the positive x‑axis:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Vₓ = V cosθ</strong></li>
        <li><strong>V_y = V sinθ</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>These components are often written as <strong>V = Vₓ î + V_y ĵ</strong>.</p>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">5.2 Three‑Dimensional Resolution</h3>
    <p>When a vector makes angles <span style="color: var(--warning);">α, β, γ</span> with the x, y, z axes respectively (direction cosines), the components are:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Vₓ = V cosα</strong></li>
        <li><strong>V_y = V cosβ</strong></li>
        <li><strong>V_z = V cosγ</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Direction cosines satisfy the identity <strong>cos²α + cos²β + cos²γ = 1</strong>.</p>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">5.3 Mnemonic for Quick Recall</h3>
    <p><em>“C‑O‑S for Cosine, S‑I‑N for Sine – multiply magnitude, keep sign!”</em> <br>Remember: <strong>Component = Magnitude × (C)osine (for adjacent side) or (S)ine (for opposite side).</strong></p>
    
    <h2 style="color:#34495e;">6. Dot (Scalar) Product</h2>
    <p>The dot product of two vectors yields a scalar. It is central to work, projection, and angle calculations.</p>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">6.1 Definition</h3>
    <p>For vectors **A** and **B** with an included angle <span style="color: var(--warning);">θ</span>:</p>
    <p><strong>**A**·**B** = |A| |B| cosθ</strong></p>
    <p>In component form (Cartesian coordinates):</p>
    <pre style="background:#eef; padding:10px; border-left:4px solid #e67e22;">
A·B = AₓBₓ + A_yB_y + A_zB_z
    </pre>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">6.2 Physical Meaning &amp; Applications</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Work Done (W)</strong> by a constant force **F** moving an object through displacement **s**: <strong>W = F·s = |F||s|cosθ</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Projection of A on B</strong>: <strong>proj_B A = (A·B / |B|²) B</strong>.</li>
        <li>Determining orthogonality: <strong>A·B = 0 ⇔ A ⟂ B</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">6.3 Properties (Exam‑Friendly)</h3>
    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background:#e2e8f0;">
                <th style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Property</th>
                <th style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Mathematical Form</th>
                <th style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Implication</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Commutative</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">A·B = B·A</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Order does not matter.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Distributive over addition</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">A·(B + C) = A·B + A·C</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Useful for breaking complex vectors.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Scalar multiplication</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">(kA)·B = k(A·B)</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Factor out constants.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Self‑product</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">A·A = |A|²</td>
                <td style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:8px;">Gives magnitude squared.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">6.4 Mnemonic for Dot Product</h3>
    <p><em>“DOT = D O T → D = “Only” T = “Together” → gives a <strong>Scalar</strong>”</em></p>
    
    <h2 style="color:#34495e;">7. Cross (Vector) Product</h2>
    <p>The cross product of two vectors yields a third vector that is perpendicular to the plane containing the original vectors.</p>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">7.1 Definition</h3>
    <p>For vectors **A** and **B** with angle <span style="color: var(--warning);">θ</span> (0 ≤ θ ≤ π):</p>
    <p><strong>**A** × **B** = |A| |B| sinθ **n̂**</strong></p>
    <p>where **n̂** is a unit vector perpendicular to the plane of **A** and **B**, determined by the <strong>right‑hand rule</strong>.</p>
    
    <h3 style="color:#2c3e50;">7.2 Component (Determinant) Form</h3>
    <pre style="background:#eef; padding:10px; border-left:4px solid #27ae60;">
A × B = | î   ĵ   k̂ |
        | Aₓ  A
`;

