window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-geography-interior-of-earth-layers-discontinuities-seismic-waves"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h1 style="text-align: center; color: #2C3E50;">The Interior of Earth: Layers, Discontinuities, and Seismic Waves</h1>
    <p>Understanding the Earth's interior is crucial for comprehending geological phenomena like earthquakes, volcanism, and plate tectonics. Since direct observation is extremely limited, scientists rely heavily on indirect methods, primarily the study of seismic waves.</p>

    <h2 style="color: #34495E;">1. Sources of Information about Earth's Interior</h2>
    <p>Our knowledge of the Earth's deep interior comes from both direct and indirect sources:</p>

    <h3 style="color: #4A627A;">1.1. Direct Sources</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Mining and Drilling Projects:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Deepest Mines:</strong> The Mponeng Gold Mine in South Africa reaches depths of approximately <span style='color: var(--warning);'>4 km</span>.</li>
                <li><strong>Deepest Drillings:</strong> The Kola Superdeep Borehole in Russia reached a depth of <span style='color: var(--warning);'>12.2 km</span> (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>1989</span>). These depths are minuscule compared to the Earth's radius of approximately <span style='color: var(--warning);'>6371 km</span>.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Volcanic Eruptions:</strong> Bring molten material (magma) from depths of up to <span style='color: var(--warning);'>100-200 km</span> to the surface. This provides clues about the composition of the upper mantle.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="color: #4A627A;">1.2. Indirect Sources (Most Important)</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Seismic Waves:</strong> The most important source. The study of earthquake waves (seismology) provides a detailed picture of the Earth's layered structure. Changes in the velocity and direction of seismic waves indicate changes in density, temperature, and physical state (solid, liquid, plastic) of the material they pass through.</li>
        <li><strong>Meteorites:</strong> These extraterrestrial bodies are believed to be remnants of a planet that broke apart, similar in composition to Earth's core. They provide insights into the potential composition of the Earth's interior (e.g., iron-nickel).</li>
        <li><strong>Gravity Anomalies:</strong> Variations in gravity at different locations on Earth indicate an uneven distribution of mass within the Earth's interior. Positive anomalies suggest excess mass, while negative anomalies suggest a deficit of mass.</li>
        <li><strong>Magnetic Field:</strong> The Earth's magnetic field is generated by the convective motion of molten iron and nickel in the outer core (dynamo effect). Studying its properties helps infer the state and composition of the outer core.</li>
        <li><strong>Temperature and Pressure:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Temperature:</strong> Increases with depth at a rate of approximately <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1°C per 32 meters</span> in the upper crust (geothermal gradient). This rate decreases significantly deeper down. The core is estimated to be as hot as the sun's surface (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>~5000-6000°C</span>).</li>
                <li><strong>Pressure:</strong> Increases significantly with depth due to the weight of overlying material. This immense pressure keeps the inner core solid despite extremely high temperatures.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Density:</strong> Increases with depth, from an average of <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2.7-3.0 g/cm³</span> in the crust to over <span style='color: var(--warning);'>13 g/cm³</span> in the inner core. This increase is inferred from seismic wave velocities and gravitational studies.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="color: #34495E;">2. Seismic Waves: The Key to Earth's Interior</h2>
    <p>Earthquakes generate different types of seismic waves that travel through the Earth. Their behavior (speed, refraction, reflection) provides crucial information.</p>

    <h3 style="color: #4A627A;">2.1. Earthquake Terminology</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Focus (Hypocenter):</strong> The point within the Earth where the earthquake rupture originates.</li>
        <li><strong>Epicenter:</strong> The point on the Earth's surface directly above the focus. It is usually the point of maximum intensity.</li>
        <li><strong>Seismograph:</strong> An instrument used to detect and record ground motion caused by seismic waves.</li>
        <li><strong>Seismogram:</strong> The record (graph) produced by a seismograph, showing the arrival times and amplitudes of different seismic waves.</li>
        <li><strong>Isoseismal Lines:</strong> Lines connecting points of equal earthquake intensity on a map.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="color: #4A627A;">2.2. Types of Seismic Waves</h3>
    <p>Seismic waves are broadly classified into Body Waves and Surface Waves.</p>

    <h4>2.2.1. Body Waves</h4>
    <p>Travel through the interior of the Earth. They are faster than surface waves.</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>P-waves (Primary or Pressure Waves):</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Nature:</strong> Longitudinal waves (compressional waves), similar to sound waves. Particles oscillate back and forth in the direction of wave propagation.</li>
                <li><strong>Speed:</strong> Fastest of all seismic waves, arriving first at seismographs. Their speed varies with the density and elasticity of the medium, increasing with depth.</li>
                <li><strong>Medium:</strong> Can travel through <strong>solids, liquids, and gases</strong>.</li>
                <li><strong>Effect:</strong> Cause volume changes in the material.</li>
                <li><strong>Destructive Power:</strong> Least destructive among seismic waves.</li>
                <li><strong>Analogy:</strong> Pushing and pulling a Slinky toy.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>S-waves (Secondary or Shear Waves):</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Nature:</strong> Transverse waves. Particles oscillate perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation.</li>
                <li><strong>Speed:</strong> Slower than P-waves, arriving second at seismographs. Approximately <span style='color: var(--warning);'>60%</span> of P-wave speed.</li>
                <li><strong>Medium:</strong> Can travel only through <strong>solids</strong>. They cannot pass through liquids or gases because these media cannot sustain shear stress. This property is crucial for determining the liquid nature of the outer core.</li>
                <li><strong>Effect:</strong> Cause shape changes in the material.</li>
                <li><strong>Destructive Power:</strong> More destructive than P-waves.</li>
                <li><strong>Analogy:</strong> Shaking a rope up and down.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h4>2.2.2. Surface Waves (L-waves)</h4>
    <p>Travel along the Earth's surface and are responsible for most of the damage during an earthquake.</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Love Waves (L-waves):</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Nature:</strong> Horizontal shear waves. Particles move horizontally, perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation.</li>
                <li><strong>Speed:</strong> Slower than S-waves.</li>
                <li><strong>Medium:</strong> Travel along the surface.</li>
                <li><strong>Destructive Power:</strong> Highly destructive, causing horizontal shearing of the ground.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Rayleigh Waves (R-waves):</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Nature:</strong> Elliptical or rolling motion, similar to ocean waves. Particles move in an elliptical path, both horizontally and vertically.</li>
                <li><strong>Speed:</strong> Slowest of all seismic waves.</li>
                <li><strong>Medium:</strong> Travel along the surface.</li>
                <li><strong>Destructive Power:</strong> Highly destructive, causing vertical and horizontal ground motion.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="table-responsive">
        <h4 style="color: #4A627A;">Comparison of Seismic Waves</h4>
        <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Wave Type</th>
                    <th>Nature</th>
                    <th>Medium of Travel</th>
                    <th>Relative Speed</th>
                    <th>Particle Motion</th>
                    <th>Destructive Power</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>P-waves</strong></td>
                    <td>Longitudinal (Compressional)</td>
                    <td>Solid, Liquid, Gas</td>
                    <td>Fastest</td>
                    <td>Parallel to wave direction</td>
                    <td>Least</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>S-waves</strong></td>
                    <td>Transverse (Shear)</td>
                    <td>Solid only</td>
                    <td>Slower than P</td>
                    <td>Perpendicular to wave direction</td>
                    <td>Moderate</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Love Waves</strong></td>
                    <td>Horizontal Shear</td>
                    <td>Surface</td>
                    <td>Slower than S</td>
                    <td>Horizontal, perpendicular to wave direction</td>
                    <td>High</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Rayleigh Waves</strong></td>
                    <td>Elliptical/Rolling</td>
                    <td>Surface</td>
                    <td>Slowest</td>
                    <td>Elliptical/Circular</td>
                    <td>Highest</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3 style="color: #4A627A;">2.3. Seismic Shadow Zones</h3>
    <p>The existence of shadow zones for P-waves and S-waves provided the first strong evidence for the Earth's layered structure, particularly the liquid outer core.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>S-wave Shadow Zone:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Location:</strong> Extends beyond <span style='color: var(--warning);'>103°</span> from the epicenter, covering nearly half of the Earth's surface opposite the epicenter.</li>
                <li><strong>Reason:</strong> S-waves cannot travel through liquids. When S-waves encounter the liquid outer core, they are completely absorbed. This proves that the outer core is liquid.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>P-wave Shadow Zone:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Location:</strong> A band between <span style='color: var(--warning);'>103°</span> and <span style='color: var(--warning);'>142°</span> from the epicenter.</li>
                <li><strong>Reason:</strong> P-waves can travel through the liquid outer core, but their velocity significantly decreases, and they are strongly refracted (bent) at the mantle-outer core boundary and again at the outer core-inner core boundary. This refraction causes them to be deflected away from the shadow zone. The existence of this shadow zone indicates a significant change in density and state at the core-mantle boundary.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the S-wave shadow zone is larger and more complete, directly proving the liquid outer core. The
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-geography-rocks-minerals-igneous-sedimentary-metamorphic-rock-cycle"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h1>Rocks & Minerals – Igneous, Sedimentary, Metamorphic; Rock Cycle</h1>

    <p>Understanding rocks and minerals is fundamental to comprehending Earth's geological processes, landforms, and resource distribution. This topic is crucial for defence exams like NDA, CDS, and AFCAT, often featuring questions on rock types, their formation, characteristics, and economic significance.</p>

    <h2>1. Minerals: The Building Blocks of Rocks</h2>
    <p>A <strong>mineral</strong> is a naturally occurring, inorganic solid with a definite chemical composition and a characteristic crystalline structure. Rocks are aggregates of one or more minerals.</p>

    <h3>1.1. Key Characteristics of Minerals:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Naturally Occurring:</strong> Formed by natural geological processes, not synthetic.</li>
        <li><strong>Inorganic:</strong> Generally not formed from living organisms or organic compounds (exceptions exist, e.g., some biogenic minerals).</li>
        <li><strong>Solid:</strong> Must be in a solid state at normal temperatures and pressures.</li>
        <li><strong>Definite Chemical Composition:</strong> Can be expressed by a chemical formula (e.g., Quartz: SiO₂, Halite: NaCl). This composition may vary within specific limits.</li>
        <li><strong>Ordered Atomic Arrangement (Crystalline Structure):</strong> Atoms are arranged in a highly ordered, repetitive manner, forming a crystal lattice. This internal structure determines many physical properties.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>1.2. Physical Properties of Minerals (for Identification):</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Color:</strong> Often the most obvious but least reliable property due to impurities (e.g., quartz can be clear, white, pink, purple).</li>
        <li><strong>Streak:</strong> The color of a mineral in its powdered form, obtained by rubbing it on an unglazed porcelain plate (streak plate). More reliable than color (e.g., hematite is red-brown, but its streak is consistently reddish-brown).</li>
        <li><strong>Luster:</strong> Describes how light reflects off a mineral's surface.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Metallic:</strong> Looks like polished metal (e.g., Galena, Pyrite).</li>
                <li><strong>Non-metallic:</strong>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Vitreous/Glassy:</strong> Like glass (e.g., Quartz, Feldspar).</li>
                        <li><strong>Pearly:</strong> Like a pearl (e.g., Talc, Muscovite Mica).</li>
                        <li><strong>Silky:</strong> Like silk (e.g., Asbestos, Gypsum).</li>
                        <li><strong>Resinous:</strong> Like resin (e.g., Sphalerite).</li>
                        <li><strong>Greasy/Oily:</strong> Appears to be covered in a thin layer of oil (e.g., Nepheline).</li>
                        <li><strong>Dull/Earthy:</strong> Non-reflective, like dirt (e.g., Kaolinite).</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Hardness:</strong> A mineral's resistance to scratching. Measured using the <strong>Mohs Scale of Hardness</strong> (a relative scale from <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1</span> to <span style='color: var(--warning);'>10</span>).
            <br/>
            <strong>Mohs Scale Mnemonic:</strong> "<strong>T</strong>o <strong>G</strong>et <strong>C</strong>andy <strong>F</strong>rom <strong>A</strong>unt <strong>F</strong>anny, <strong>Q</strong>uit <strong>T</strong>aking <strong>C</strong>andy <strong>D</strong>ishes."
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Talc</strong> (softest)</li>
                <li><strong>Gypsum</strong></li>
                <li><strong>Calcite</strong></li>
                <li><strong>Fluorite</strong></li>
                <li><strong>Apatite</strong></li>
                <li><strong>Orthoclase Feldspar</strong></li>
                <li><strong>Quartz</strong></li>
                <li><strong>Topaz</strong></li>
                <li><strong>Corundum</strong></li>
                <li><strong>Diamond</strong> (hardest)</li>
            </ol>
            Common reference points: Fingernail (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>2.5</span>), Copper coin (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>3.5</span>), Steel knife (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>5.5</span>), Glass (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>5.5-6</span>).
        </li>
        <li><strong>Cleavage:</strong> The tendency of a mineral to break along smooth, flat surfaces (planes of weakness) determined by its atomic structure. Described by the number of directions and the angle between them (e.g., <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1</span> direction - mica; <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2</span> directions at <span style='color: var(--warning);'>90°</span> - feldspar; <span style='color: var(--warning);'>3</span> directions at <span style='color: var(--warning);'>90°</span> - halite; <span style='color: var(--warning);'>3</span> directions not at <span style='color: var(--warning);'>90°</span> - calcite).</li>
        <li><strong>Fracture:</strong> The way a mineral breaks when it does not exhibit cleavage.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Conchoidal:</strong> Smooth, curved surfaces like broken glass (e.g., Quartz, Obsidian).</li>
                <li><strong>Fibrous/Splintery:</strong> Breaks into splinters (e.g., Asbestos).</li>
                <li><strong>Uneven/Irregular:</strong> Rough, irregular surfaces.</li>
                <li><strong>Earthy:</strong> Crumbly, like clay.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Crystal Form/Habit:</strong> The external shape of a mineral crystal, reflecting its internal atomic arrangement, if allowed to grow unimpeded (e.g., cubic, hexagonal, prismatic).</li>
        <li><strong>Specific Gravity (Density):</strong> The ratio of a mineral's density to the density of water. Helps distinguish between minerals of similar appearance.</li>
        <li><strong>Other Properties:</strong> Magnetism (e.g., Magnetite), Reaction to acid (e.g., Calcite effervesces with HCl), Taste (e.g., Halite is salty), Odor (e.g., Sulfur), Feel (e.g., Talc is soapy).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>1.3. Classification of Minerals (Major Groups):</h3>
    <p>Minerals are primarily classified based on their chemical composition, specifically the anion or anionic group they contain.</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Silicates (SiO₄⁻⁴):</strong> Most abundant mineral group, forming over <span style='color: var(--warning);'>90%</span> of Earth's crust. Based on the silicon-oxygen tetrahedron.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Feldspars:</strong> Most common mineral group. Orthoclase (K-feldspar) and Plagioclase (Na-Ca feldspar).</li>
                <li><strong>Quartz:</strong> SiO₂, very common, hard, resistant to weathering.</li>
                <li><strong>Micas:</strong> Muscovite (light) and Biotite (dark). Excellent cleavage in one direction.</li>
                <li><strong>Amphiboles:</strong> Hornblende is common. Double chain silicates.</li>
                <li><strong>Pyroxenes:</strong> Augite is common. Single chain silicates.</li>
                <li><strong>Olivine:</strong> High temperature, dark green mineral. Independent tetrahedra.</li>
                <li><strong>Garnet:</strong> Hard, common in metamorphic rocks.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Oxides (O⁻²):</strong> Minerals containing oxygen bonded with a metal.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Hematite (Fe₂O₃):</strong> Important iron ore.</li>
                <li><strong>Magnetite (Fe₃O₄):</strong> Magnetic iron ore.</li>
                <li><strong>Corundum (Al₂O₃):</strong> Very hard, includes ruby and sapphire.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Carbonates (CO₃⁻²):</strong> Minerals containing the carbonate ion.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Calcite (CaCO₃):</strong> Main component of limestone and marble. Reacts with acid.</li>
                <li><strong>Dolomite (CaMg(CO₃)₂):</strong> Similar to calcite, but reacts weakly with acid.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Sulfides (S⁻²):</strong> Minerals containing sulfur bonded with a metal. Often important ore minerals.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Pyrite (FeS₂):</strong> "Fool's Gold."</li>
                <li><strong>Galena (PbS):</strong> Lead ore.</li>
                <li><strong>Sphalerite (ZnS):</strong> Zinc ore.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Sulfates (SO₄⁻²):</strong> Minerals containing the sulfate ion.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Gypsum (CaSO₄·2H₂O):</strong> Used in plaster and drywall.</li>
                <li><strong>Anhydrite (CaSO₄):</strong> Anhydrous form of gypsum.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Halides (Cl⁻, F⁻, Br⁻, I⁻):</strong> Minerals containing a halogen element.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Halite (NaCl):</strong> Table salt.</li>
                <li><strong>Fluorite (CaF₂):</strong> Used in steelmaking.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Native Elements:</strong> Minerals composed of a single element.
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Gold (Au), Silver (Ag), Copper (Cu), Diamond (C), Graphite (C), Sulfur (S).</strong></li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>2. Rocks: Aggregates of Minerals</h2>
    <p>A <strong>rock</strong> is a naturally occurring solid aggregate of one or more minerals or mineraloids. Rocks are classified into three major types based on their mode of formation:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Igneous Rocks:</strong> Formed from the cooling and solidification of molten rock (magma or lava).</li>
        <li><strong>Sedimentary Rocks:</strong> Formed from the accumulation and lithification of sediments (weathered rock fragments, organic matter, or chemical precipitates).</li>
        <li><strong>Metamorphic Rocks:</strong> Formed when existing rocks are transformed by heat, pressure, or chemically active fluids without melting.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>3. Igneous Rocks</h2>
    <p>Igneous rocks (from Latin <em>ignis</em>, meaning fire) are formed from the cooling and solidification of molten material called <strong>magma</strong> (below
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-geography-plate-tectonics-plates-boundaries-sea-floor-spreading"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h2 style="text-align: center; color: var(--primary);">Plate Tectonics – Plates, Boundaries, Sea-Floor Spreading</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>Plate Tectonics</strong> is a unifying theory in geology that explains the large-scale motion of Earth's lithosphere. It describes the movement of large plates of the Earth's crust and upper mantle, and the geological phenomena that result from these movements, such as earthquakes, volcanoes, and mountain building. This theory revolutionized our understanding of Earth's dynamic processes.
    </p>

    <h3><span style="color: var(--secondary);">1. Historical Context and Evolution of the Theory</span></h3>

    <h4>1.1. Continental Drift Hypothesis</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Proposed by German meteorologist <strong>Alfred Wegener</strong> in <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1912</span> (published in <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1915</span>).</li>
        <li><strong>Core Idea:</strong> Continents were once joined together in a supercontinent called <strong>Pangaea</strong> (meaning "all-earth") approximately <span style='color: var(--warning);'>200-300 million years ago</span>, and have since drifted apart.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence Presented by Wegener:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Jigsaw Fit of Continents:</strong> The remarkable fit of the coastlines of South America and Africa.</li>
                <li><strong>Paleontological Evidence (Fossil Distribution):</strong> Identical fossil species (e.g., <em>Mesosaurus</em>, <em>Lystrosaurus</em>, <em>Glossopteris</em> flora) found on continents now separated by vast oceans, suggesting they were once connected.</li>
                <li><strong>Geological Evidence (Rock Types and Structures):</strong> Similar rock formations, mountain ranges (e.g., Appalachian Mountains in North America and Caledonian Mountains in Europe), and mineral deposits found across continents that would align if reassembled.</li>
                <li><strong>Paleoclimatic Evidence:</strong> Evidence of ancient climates (e.g., glacial deposits in tropical regions like India and Australia, coal deposits in Antarctica) that are inconsistent with their current latitudes, suggesting continents have moved.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Limitations:</strong> Wegener could not provide a plausible mechanism for how continents could "drift" through the solid oceanic crust. His proposed forces (tidal forces, centrifugal force) were deemed insufficient by the scientific community, leading to initial rejection of his hypothesis.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>1.2. Convection Current Hypothesis</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Proposed by British geologist <strong>Arthur Holmes</strong> in <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1929</span>.</li>
        <li><strong>Core Idea:</strong> Suggested that convection currents in the Earth's mantle could provide the driving force for continental drift. Hot, less dense material rises from the core-mantle boundary, spreads laterally beneath the lithosphere, cools, and then sinks, creating a continuous cycle.</li>
        <li>This hypothesis provided a crucial missing piece for Wegener's theory but was not widely accepted until further evidence emerged decades later.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>1.3. Sea-Floor Spreading Hypothesis</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Developed by American geophysicist <strong>Harry Hess</strong> in <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1960</span> (published in <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1962</span>) and refined by <strong>Robert Dietz</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Core Idea:</strong> New oceanic crust is continuously generated at <strong>mid-oceanic ridges</strong> (divergent plate boundaries) as magma rises from the mantle. This new crust then moves away from the ridge in both directions, carrying the continents with it, and is eventually consumed at <strong>oceanic trenches</strong> (convergent plate boundaries) through a process called <strong>subduction</strong>.</li>
        <li>This hypothesis provided the actual mechanism for the movement of continents and the growth/destruction of ocean basins.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>1.4. Emergence of Plate Tectonics</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>The synthesis of Continental Drift, Convection Currents, and Sea-Floor Spreading, along with new evidence from paleomagnetism and seismic studies, led to the development of the comprehensive theory of <strong>Plate Tectonics</strong> in the late <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1960s</span>.</li>
        <li>It explains virtually all major geological features and processes on Earth.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3><span style="color: var(--secondary);">2. Earth's Plates (Lithospheric Plates)</span></h3>

    <h4>2.1. Definition of a Plate</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>A <strong>plate</strong> is a large, rigid slab of Earth's <strong>lithosphere</strong> (crust and uppermost solid mantle) that moves as a single unit over the semi-fluid <strong>asthenosphere</strong> (a weaker, ductile layer of the upper mantle).</li>
        <li>Plates can be composed entirely of <strong>oceanic crust</strong>, entirely of <strong>continental crust</strong>, or a combination of both.</li>
        <li>The thickness of plates varies: oceanic plates are typically <span style='color: var(--warning);'>5-10 km</span> thick (crust) plus <span style='color: var(--warning);'>60-90 km</span> of mantle, while continental plates are much thicker, ranging from <span style='color: var(--warning);'>30-70 km</span> (crust) plus <span style='color: var(--warning);'>100-200 km</span> of mantle.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>2.2. Major and Minor Plates</h4>
    <p>There are <span style='color: var(--warning);'>7-8</span> major plates and numerous minor plates.</p>

    <h5>Major Plates:</h5>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Plate Name</th>
                <th>Composition</th>
                <th>General Location</th>
                <th>Key Features/Movement</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Pacific Plate</strong></td>
                <td>Mostly Oceanic</td>
                <td>Underlies the Pacific Ocean</td>
                <td>Largest plate; moving northwest; surrounded by subduction zones (Ring of Fire).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>North American Plate</strong></td>
                <td>Continental & Oceanic</td>
                <td>North America, western North Atlantic, Greenland</td>
                <td>Moving west-southwest; divergent boundary with Eurasian and African plates; convergent with Pacific plate.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>South American Plate</strong></td>
                <td>Continental & Oceanic</td>
                <td>South America, western South Atlantic</td>
                <td>Moving west; divergent boundary with African plate; convergent with Nazca plate.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Eurasian Plate</strong></td>
                <td>Continental & Oceanic</td>
                <td>Europe, Asia (excluding Indian subcontinent), eastern North Atlantic</td>
                <td>Moving east-southeast; complex interactions with African, Indian, and Pacific plates.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>African Plate</strong></td>
                <td>Continental & Oceanic</td>
                <td>Africa, eastern South Atlantic, western Indian Ocean</td>
                <td>Moving northeast; divergent boundaries with South American and Arabian plates; complex convergent with Eurasian plate.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Indo-Australian Plate</strong></td>
                <td>Continental & Oceanic</td>
                <td>India, Australia, Indian Ocean</td>
                <td>Moving northeast; convergent boundary with Eurasian plate (Himalayas); divergent with Antarctic plate. (Sometimes considered two separate plates: Indian and Australian).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Antarctic Plate</strong></td>
                <td>Continental & Oceanic</td>
                <td>Antarctica, surrounding Southern Ocean</td>
                <td>Moving generally north; surrounded by divergent boundaries.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h5>Minor Plates (Examples):</h5>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Nazca Plate:</strong> Oceanic plate off the west coast of South America, subducting under the South American Plate (responsible for Andes Mountains).</li>
        <li><strong>Cocos Plate:</strong> Oceanic plate off the west coast of Central America, subducting under the North American and Caribbean plates.</li>
        <li><strong>Caribbean Plate:</strong> Mostly oceanic, underlies the Caribbean Sea.</li>
        <li><strong>Philippine Sea Plate:</strong> Oceanic plate east of the Philippines, subducting under the Eurasian plate.</li>
        <li><strong>Arabian Plate:</strong> Mostly continental, Arabian Peninsula, moving northeast, colliding with Eurasian plate.</li>
        <li><strong>Juan de Fuca Plate:</strong> Small oceanic plate off the Pacific Northwest of North America, subducting under the North American Plate.</li>
        <li><strong>Scotia Plate:</strong> Small oceanic plate between South America and Antarctica.</li>
        <li><strong>Fiji Plate:</strong> Complex small plate in the southwest Pacific.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>2.3. Forces Driving Plate Movement</h4>
    <p>The primary forces responsible for plate motion are:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Convection Currents (Mantle Convection):</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>The most widely accepted primary driver. Heat from the Earth's core and mantle causes material in the asthenosphere to become less dense and rise. As it reaches the lithosphere, it spreads horizontally, dragging the plates along. It then cools, becomes denser, and sinks back into the mantle, completing the convection cell.</li>
                <li>This process is analogous to boiling water in a pot.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Ridge Push:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Occurs at <strong>mid-oceanic ridges</strong> (divergent boundaries). As new, hot, buoyant crust forms at the ridge, it stands higher than the older, cooler crust away from the ridge.</li>
                <li>Gravity causes this elevated ridge to push the oceanic plate away from the ridge crest, down the gentle slope.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Slab Pull:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Considered the most significant force. Occurs at <strong>subduction zones</strong> (convergent boundaries).</li>
                <li>As an oceanic plate cools and becomes denser with age, it eventually becomes denser than the underlying asthenosphere. When it begins to subduct, its own weight pulls the rest of the plate down into the mantle, much like a heavy chain hanging over the edge of a table.</li>
                <li>The steeper the angle of subduction, the stronger the slab pull.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Slab Suction:</strong> A minor force where the subducting slab creates a localized convection current in the mantle, sucking the overriding plate towards the trench.</li>
    </ul>
`;

