window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-geography-climate-zones-biomes-tropical-temperate-polar-zones"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
    <h1>World Geography – Climate Zones & Biomes</h1>
    <h2>Quick Overview (Exam‑Friendly)</h2>
    <p><strong>Climate zones</strong> are broad latitudinal belts that dictate the <strong>temperature</strong>, <strong>precipitation</strong> and consequently the <strong>biomes</strong> that develop on Earth’s surface. The three primary zones examined for NDA/CDS/AFCAT are:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Tropical Zone</strong> (≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0°–23.5°</span> N/S)</li>
        <li><strong>Temperate Zone</strong> (≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>23.5°–66.5°</span> N/S)</li>
        <li><strong>Polar Zone</strong> (≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>66.5°–90°</span> N/S)</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Mnemonic to remember the latitudinal limits: <strong>“30‑60‑90”</strong> → 30° ≈ tropics, 60° ≈ mid‑temperate, 90° ≈ pole. Another handy phrase is <strong>“TTP – Tropical, Temperate, Polar”</strong>.</p>

    <h2>1. Tropical Climate Zone</h2>
    <h3>1.1 Definition & Extent</h3>
    <p>The tropical zone lies between the <strong>Equator</strong> and the <strong>Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn</strong> (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>23.5° N</span> and <span style='color: var(--warning);'>23.5° S</span>). It receives the highest solar insolation year‑round.</p>

    <h3>1.2 Key Climatic Features</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Parameter</th>
            <th>Typical Value</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Mean Annual Temperature</strong></td>
            <td>> <span style='color: var(--warning);'>18 °C</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Temperature Range (annual)</strong></td>
            <td>± <span style='color: var(--warning);'>3–5 °C</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Rainfall</strong></td>
            <td>> <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1500 mm</span> / yr (often > <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2500 mm</span>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Seasonality</strong></td>
            <td>Two main patterns:
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Af (Equatorial)</strong> – no dry season.</li>
                    <li><strong>Aw/Am (Monsoonal)</strong> – distinct wet & dry periods.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>1.3 Dominant Biomes</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Rainforest (Tropical Evergreen)</strong> – high canopy, > <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2000 mm</span> rain, biodiversity hotspot.</li>
        <li><strong>Savanna (Tropical Grassland)</strong> – wet summer, dry winter, fire‑adapted grasses.</li>
        <li><strong>Monsoon Forest (Tropical Deciduous)</strong> – sheds leaves in dry season.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>1.4 Representative Regions & Countries</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#e8f5e9;">
            <th>Region</th>
            <th>Key Countries</th>
            <th>Typical Biome</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Amazon Basin</td>
            <td>Brazil, Peru, Colombia</td>
            <td>Rainforest</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Congo Basin</td>
            <td>DRC, Cameroon, Central African Republic</td>
            <td>Rainforest</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Indian Subcontinent (south)</td>
            <td>India, Sri Lanka, Bangladesh</td>
            <td>Monsoon Forest & Savanna</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Southeast Asia</td>
            <td>Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand</td>
            <td>Rainforest & Mangroves</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>1.5 Human Activities & Adaptations</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Intensive <strong>agriculture</strong> – rice, cassava, sugarcane.</li>
        <li>High <strong>population density</strong> in river valleys (e.g., Ganges, Amazon).</li>
        <li>Deforestation for timber & plantation crops (palm oil, rubber).</li>
        <li>Adaptations: <em>stilt houses</em> in flood‑prone areas, <em>crop rotation</em> to maintain soil fertility.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>1.6 Exam Tips</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Remember the Köppen codes: <strong>A</strong> = Tropical (Af, Am, Aw).</li>
        <li>For quick recall, link <strong>temperature > 18 °C</strong> with the word <strong>“Always Hot”</strong>.</li>
        <li>Key numbers to memorize: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>23.5°</span> (tropics), <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1500 mm</span> (minimum annual rain for rainforest).</li>
    </ul>

    <hr/>

    <h2>2. Temperate Climate Zone</h2>
    <h3>2.1 Definition & Extent</h3>
    <p>The temperate zone stretches from the Tropics of Cancer/Capricorn (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>23.5°</span>) to the Polar Circles (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>66.5°</span>) in both hemispheres. It experiences moderate solar radiation and marked seasonality.</p>

    <h3>2.2 Climatic Sub‑Types (Köppen)</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#f9fbe7;">
            <th>Köppen Symbol</th>
            <th>Name</th>
            <th>Key Features</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Cfa</td>
            <td>Humid Subtropical</td>
            <td>Hot summers, mild winters, no dry season.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Cfb</td>
            <td>Oceanic (Marine West Coast)</td>
            <td>Cool summers, mild winters, evenly distributed rain.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Csa / Csb</td>
            <td>Mediterranean</td>
            <td>Dry summer, wet winter; hot (a) or warm (b) summer.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Dfa / Dfb</td>
            <td>Humid Continental</td>
            <td>Warm‑hot summer, cold winter; no dry season.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Dwa / Dwb</td>
            <td>Continental Monsoon</td>
            <td>Hot summer, cold winter, dry winter.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>2.3 Typical Climate Parameters</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Parameter</th>
            <th>Range</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Mean Annual Temperature</strong></td>
            <td>≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>5 °C–20 °C</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Annual Temperature Amplitude</strong></td>
            <td>≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>15 °C–30 °C</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Annual Precipitation</strong></td>
            <td>≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>500 mm–1500 mm</span> (varies with sub‑type)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Seasonality</strong></td>
            <td>Four distinct seasons – spring, summer, autumn, winter.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>2.4 Dominant Biomes</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Deciduous Forest</strong> – broad‑leaf trees shed leaves in winter; rich soils.</li>
        <li><strong>Coniferous (Taiga) Forest</strong> – needle‑leaf evergreens; cold‑tolerant.</li>
        <li><strong>Mixed Forest</strong> – combination of deciduous & coniferous species.</li>
        <li><strong>Grassland (Prairie / Steppe)</strong> – moderate rainfall, fire‑adapted grasses.</li>
        <li><strong>Temperate Mediterranean Shrubland (Chaparral)</strong> – drought‑resistant sclerophyllous plants.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2.5 Representative Regions & Countries</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#e3f2fd;">
            <th>Region</th>
            <th>Countries</th>
            <th>Biome(s)</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Eastern United States</td>
            <td>USA</td>
            <td>Deciduous Forest, Temperate Grassland</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Western Europe</td>
            <td>France, Germany, UK</td>
            <td>Temperate Oceanic, Mixed Forest</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>East Asia (East of the Himalayas)</td>
            <td>China, Japan, Korea</td>
            <td>Temperate Deciduous, Mixed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Southern South America</td>
            <td>Argentina, Chile</td>
            <td>Temperate Rainforest, Mediterranean Shrubland</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Southern Africa (Highveld)</td>
            <td>South Africa, Lesotho</td>
            <td>Temperate Grassland (Savanna‑like)</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>2.6 Human Activities & Adaptations</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Intensive <strong>cereal agriculture</strong> – wheat, barley, maize.</li>
        <li>Large‑scale <strong>industrialisation</strong> – Europe, North America, East Asia.</li>
        <li>Urbanisation concentrated in river valleys and coastal plains.</li>
        <li>Adaptations: <em>seasonal clothing</em>, <em>crop rotation & fallow periods</em> to maintain soil fertility.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2.7 Mnemonics & Quick Recall</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>“<strong>COOL‑COLD‑COLD</strong>” – <strong>C</strong> for <strong>C</strong>ool summers (Cfb), <strong>O</strong> for Oceanic, <strong>O</strong> for <strong>O</strong>ther (Mediterranean), <strong>L</strong> for <strong>L</strong>and‑locked continental (Dfa/Dfb).</li>
        <li>Latitude mnemonic: <strong>“Two‑Six‑Six‑Five”</strong> → 23.5° (tropics) → 66.5° (polar). Anything between is <strong>Temperate</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2.8 Exam Tips</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Remember the Köppen letter groups: <strong>A = Tropical, C = Temperate (mild), D = Continental (cold), E = Polar</strong>.</li>
        <li>Key temperature thresholds:
            <ul>
                <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>0 °C</span> – freezing line (important for taiga).</li>
                <li><span style='color: var(--warning);'>10 °C</span> – mean temperature of warmest month for many temperate classifications.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>For biomes, link <strong>leaf‑shed = Deciduous Forest</strong>, <strong>needle‑leaf = Coniferous (Taiga)</strong>, <strong>dry summer = Mediterranean Shrubland</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr/>

    <h2>3. Polar Climate Zone</h2>
    <h3>3.1 Definition & Extent</h3>
    <p>The polar zone occupies latitudes above the Arctic and Antarctic Circles (<span style='color: var(--warning);'>66.5° N/S</span>) up to the poles. Solar radiation is extremely low, leading to perpetual cold.</p>

    <h3>3.2 Climatic Characteristics</h3>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse;">
        <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
            <th>Parameter</th>
            <th>Typical Value</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Mean Annual Temperature</strong></td>
            <td>< <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0 °C</span> (often <span style='color: var(--warning);'>‑20 °C</span> to <span style='color: var(--warning);'>‑5 °C</span>)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Warmest Month Avg.</strong></td>
            <td>≤ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>10 °C</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Precipitation</strong></td>
            <td>Low – <span style='color: var(--warning);'>100–500 mm</span> yr⁻¹ (mostly as snow)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Seasonality</strong></td>
            <td>Extreme – 6 months of continuous daylight (mid‑summer) and 6 months of darkness (mid‑winter).</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h3>3.3 Köppen Polar Codes</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>ET – Tundra</strong>: Warmest month between <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0 °C</span> and <span style='color: var(--warning);'>10 °C</span>. Thin soil, permafrost just below surface.</li>
        <li><strong>EF – Ice Cap</strong>: Warmest month < <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0 °C</span>. Permanent ice and snow cover.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.4 Dominant Biomes</h3>
`;

