window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["ai-gen-biology-excretory-system-kidney-nephron-dialysis"] = `
<div class="revision-card">
  <h1>Human Excretory System – Kidney, Nephron & Dialysis</h1>

  <h2>1. Overview of the Excretory System</h2>
  <p>The excretory system removes metabolic waste, maintains fluid‑electrolyte balance, regulates acid‑base status and blood pressure. In humans the <strong>kidney</strong> is the principal organ, supported by accessory structures (ureters, bladder, urethra) and, in clinical practice, by artificial devices such as <strong>dialysis</strong> machines.</p>

  <h2>2. Kidney – Gross Anatomy</h2>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;">
    <tr style="background:#f2f2f2;">
      <th>Structure</th>
      <th>Key Features</th>
      <th>Exam‑Relevant Details</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Renal Capsule</strong></td>
      <td>Fibrous outer covering</td>
      <td>Protects kidney; thickening seen in chronic glomerulonephritis.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Renal Cortex</strong></td>
      <td>Outer layer; contains glomeruli, proximal & distal convoluted tubules, and part of the loop of Henle.</td>
      <td>Site of <strong>filtration</strong> and most <strong>reabsorption</strong>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Renal Medulla</strong></td>
      <td>Inner pyramidal region; contains loops of Henle and collecting ducts.</td>
      <td>Creates medullary osmotic gradient – essential for urine concentration.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Renal Pelvis & Calyces</strong></td>
      <td>Funnel‑shaped collecting system leading to ureter.</td>
      <td>Obstruction here → hydronephrosis (exam tip: look for flank pain & dilated pelvis).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Blood Supply</strong></td>
      <td>Renal artery → segmental → interlobar → arcuate → cortical radiate → afferent arteriole.</td>
      <td>Renal vein drains into inferior vena cava. <span style='color: var(--warning);'>~20% of cardiac output</span> reaches kidneys.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>2.1. Important Numbers (Memorise!)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Normal kidney weight: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>~150 g</span> (adult)</li>
    <li>Number of nephrons per kidney: <span style='color: var(--warning);'>~1–1.5 million</span></li>
    <li>Glomerular filtration rate (GFR): <span style='color: var(--warning);'>~125 ml/min</span> (average adult)</li>
    <li>Renal plasma flow (RPF): <span style='color: var(--warning);'>~650 ml/min</span></li>
    <li>Filtration fraction (FF) = GFR / RPF ≈ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>0.2</span></li>
    <li>Urine output (normal): <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1–2 L/day</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Nephron – Functional Unit</h2>
  <p>The nephron is the microscopic “factory” that converts plasma into urine. It consists of a **glomerular capsule** (Bowman’s capsule) and a **renal tubule**.</p>

  <h3>3.1. Historical Milestones</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1842</strong> – <strong>William Bowman</strong> described the capsule that now bears his name.</li>
    <li><strong>1862</strong> – <strong>Rudolf Virchow</strong> coined “nephron” and identified the loop of Henle.</li>
    <li><strong>1930s</strong> – Development of the **inulin clearance** method to measure GFR.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3.2. Nephron Segments & Their Functions</h3>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
    <tr style="background:#e6f7ff;">
      <th>Segment</th>
      <th>Location</th>
      <th>Major Transport Processes</th>
      <th>Key Hormonal Regulation</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Bowman’s Capsule</strong></td>
      <td>Cortex</td>
      <td>Filtration of plasma (water, electrolytes, glucose, amino acids, urea, creatinine)</td>
      <td>None directly; glomerular hydrostatic pressure set by afferent/efferent tone.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT)</strong></td>
      <td>Cortex</td>
      <td>
        <ul>
          <li>Reabsorption: 65% Na⁺, 65% water, 100% glucose & amino acids, 80% bicarbonate.</li>
          <li>Secretion: H⁺, NH₄⁺, organic acids, drugs.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td>Na⁺/K⁺‑ATPase (basolateral) drives active transport.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Loop of Henle</strong></td>
      <td>Medulla (descending thin limb → thin ascending → thick ascending)</td>
      <td>
        <ul>
          <li>Descending limb: water reabsorption (permeable to water, not solutes).</li>
          <li>Ascending limb: Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻ active reabsorption; impermeable to water.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td>Counter‑current multiplier creates medullary osmotic gradient.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT)</strong></td>
      <td>Cortex</td>
      <td>
        <ul>
          <li>Reabsorption: 5–10% Na⁺, Ca²⁺ (via TRPV5), Mg²⁺.</li>
          <li>Secretion: K⁺, H⁺ (acidification).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td>Regulated by <strong>aldosterone</strong> (Na⁺ reabsorption, K⁺ secretion) and <strong>parathyroid hormone (PTH)</strong> (Ca²⁺ reabsorption).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Collecting Duct (CD)</strong></td>
      <td>Medulla to Cortex</td>
      <td>
        <ul>
          <li>Final water reabsorption (regulated).</li>
          <li>Urea recycling to maintain medullary gradient.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>ADH (vasopressin)</strong> – inserts aquaporin‑2 channels → water reabsorption.</li>
          <li><strong>Aldosterone</strong> – enhances Na⁺ reabsorption via ENaC.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3.3. Key Equations & Mnemonics</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>GFR (Glomerular Filtration Rate)</strong> – <em>Starling’s equation for filtration</em>:
      <br><strong>GFR = Kf × (P<sub>gc</sub> – P<sub>bs</sub> – π<sub>gc</sub>)</strong>
      <br>where <strong>Kf</strong> = filtration coefficient, <strong>P<sub>gc</sub></strong> = glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, <strong>P<sub>bs</sub></strong> = Bowman's capsule hydrostatic pressure, <strong>π<sub>gc</sub></strong> = glomerular oncotic pressure.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Renal Clearance (C<sub>x</sub>)</strong> – <em>Mnemonic “RATS”</em>:
      <br><strong>R</strong>ate = (U<sub>x</sub> × V) / P<sub>x</sub>
      <br>U<sub>x</sub> = urine concentration of substance X, V = urine flow rate, P<sub>x</sub> = plasma concentration.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Creatinine Clearance</strong> – practical estimate of GFR:
      <br><strong>C<sub>Cr</sub> (ml/min) = (U<sub>Cr</sub> × V) / P<sub>Cr</sub></strong>
      <br>Remember: <strong>U<sub>Cr</sub> ≈ P<sub>Cr</sub></strong> in steady state, so C<sub>Cr</sub> ≈ urine flow × (U<sub>Cr</sub>/P<sub>Cr</sub>).</li>
    <li><strong>Fractional Excretion of Sodium (FENa)</strong> – helps differentiate prerenal vs. intrinsic AKI:
      <br><strong>FENa = [(U<sub>Na</sub> × P<sub>Cr</sub>) / (P<sub>Na</sub> × U<sub>Cr</sub>)] × 100%</strong>
      <br>Values < <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1%</span> → prerenal; > <span style='color: var(--warning);'>2%</span> → intrinsic.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3.4. Comparative Summary – Nephron vs. Other Excretory Units</h3>
  <table border="1" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
    <tr style="background:#fff0e6;">
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>Nephron (Human)</th>
      <th>Malpighian Tubule (Insect)</th>
      <th>Nephridium (Annelid)</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Primary Filtration Site</td>
      <td>Bowman’s capsule (glomerulus)</td>
      <td>Midgut epithelium</td>
      <td>Blood sinus</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Water Reabsorption Mechanism</td>
      <td>Aquaporins regulated by ADH</td>
      <td>Passive diffusion</td>
      <td>Active transport via nephridial cells</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Concentration Ability</td>
      <td>Up to 1,200 mOsm/kg (medulla gradient)</td>
      <td>Limited – excretes dilute urine</td>
      <td>Moderate – depends on segmental specialization</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>4. Regulation of Kidney Function</h2>
  <h3>4.1. Hormonal Control</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH)</strong> – secreted by posterior pituitary in response to ↑ plasma osmolality or ↓ blood volume. Increases water permeability of the collecting duct via aquaporin‑2 insertion.</li>
    <li><strong>Aldosterone</strong> – released from adrenal cortex (zona glomerulosa) when RAAS is activated. Promotes Na⁺ reabsorption (ENaC) and K⁺ secretion in DCT & CD.</li>
    <li><strong>Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP)</strong> – released from atrial myocytes when atrial stretch occurs. Dilates afferent arteriole, constricts efferent, reduces Na⁺ reabsorption → natriuresis.</li>
    <li><strong>Parathyroid Hormone (PTH)</strong> – increases Ca²⁺ reabsorption in DCT and stimulates conversion of 25‑OH vitamin D to active 1,25‑OH₂ D₃ in kidneys.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4.2. Autonomic & Local Regulation</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sympathetic nerves</strong> → vasoconstriction of afferent arteriole → ↓ GFR (fight‑or‑flight response).</li>
    <li><strong>Prostaglandins (PGI₂, PGE₂)</strong> → vasodilation of afferent arteriole; important in NSAID‑induced AKI.</li>
    <li><strong>Renin‑Angiotensin‑Aldosterone System (RAAS)</strong> – Renin (juxtaglomerular cells) converts angiotensinogen → Ang I → Ang II (via ACE). Ang II constricts efferent arteriole (↑ GFR) and stimulates aldosterone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Urine Formation – Step‑by‑Step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Glomerular Filtration</strong> – ~180 L/day of plasma filtrate formed.</li>
    <li><strong>Reabsorption</strong> – ~99% of filtered water and solutes reclaimed; primarily in PCT and loop of Henle.</li>
    <li><strong>Secretion</strong> – additional waste (H⁺, K⁺, drugs) added in PCT & DCT.</li>
    <li><strong>Excretion</strong> – final urine (~1–2 L/day) collected in renal pelvis.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>5.1. Water Balance Equation (Exam Tip)</h3>
  <p><strong>Intake – Output = ΔBody Water</strong></p>
  <p>Where Output = Urine + Insensible loss + Sweat + Respiratory loss. Remember normal urine output <span style='color: var(--warning);'>1–2 L/day</span>.</p>

  <h2>6. Dialysis – Artificial Kidney</h2>
  <p>Dialysis replaces renal clearance when kidneys fail. Two main modalities are <strong>Hemodialysis (HD)</strong> and <strong>Peritoneal Dialysis (PD)**.</p>

  <h3>6.1. Hemodialysis</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Principle</strong> – Diffusion across a semi‑permeable membrane (dialyzer) driven by concentration gradients; ultrafiltration removes excess water by hydrostatic pressure.</li>
    <li><strong>Key Components</strong>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dialyzer (Artificial Kidney)</strong> – bundle of hollow fibers (usually polysulfone). Pore size ~ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>5–10 nm</span> allows urea, creatinine, electrolytes to pass, retains proteins.</li>
        <li><strong>Dialysate</strong> – composition mimics plasma; typical concentrations: Na⁺ <span style='color: var(--warning);'>140 mEq/L</span>, K
`;

